Pterosaurs came in a huge range of sizes. The smallest had a wingspan shorter than a school ruler. Quetzalcoatlus, found in Texas, stood about as tall as a giraffe on the ground, with a head about as long as a grown-up is tall.

How did something so big take off? Many scientists think giants launched like a pole-vaulter, pushing off with their strong arms and leaping into the air. Bats use a similar push-off today.

{{story:bats-fly}}

In the 1800s, the Great Trigonometrical Survey of India measured mountains from far away, using careful [[@geometry-everyday|angles]] and a lot of arithmetic. In 1852, Radhanath Sikdar, a Bengali mathematician working for the survey, calculated that a peak listed as "Peak XV" was the highest in the world.

It was later named after George Everest, a former head of the survey, even though he had little to do with measuring it and reportedly objected to the honour. The first people known to stand on top were Tenzing Norgay and Edmund Hillary, in 1953, as told in [[@everest-first|the first Everest climb]].

{{story:everest-first}}

Sonic booms aren't only made by jets. When a whip cracks, its tip travels faster than sound for a split second, making a mini boom. Bullets from many rifles fly faster than sound too, making a crack as they pass.

In 2012, the skydiver Felix Baumgartner jumped from a balloon about 39 km up and fell faster than sound in the thin air. Thunder works in a related way: lightning heats air so fast that it expands with a shock wave, which is why [[@thunder-sound|thunder]] can crack so sharply nearby.

{{story:thunder-sound}}

The [[@fastest-animal|peregrine falcon]] hunts other birds by diving on them from high above, folding its wings into a teardrop shape. Measurements suggest it can top 300 km/h, faster than many bullet trains run.

On land, the cheetah holds the record, at around 100 km/h. In 2012, a cheetah named Sarah at the Cincinnati Zoo in the United States ran 100 m in 5.95 seconds. India's wild cheetahs disappeared in the 1950s, and in 2022, cheetahs were brought from Africa to [[@cheetah-return|Kuno National Park]] in Madhya Pradesh.

{{story:blue-whale}}

In 1889, a German scientist named Franz Müller-Lyer published a drawing of two equal lines. One had arrowheads pointing out at its ends. The other had arrowheads pointing in. Almost everyone sees the second line as longer.

One popular explanation is that the brain reads the arrowheads as the corners of a building or a room, near or far, and adjusts the size it thinks the line must be. Researchers in the 1960s found the illusion seemed weaker for some people who grew up in places with fewer straight-edged buildings. That idea is still argued about, but it suggests illusions may depend partly on where we live.

{{story:perspective}}

False stories spread faster than true ones, because they are often more surprising. In 2018, rumours forwarded on phones in parts of India led to attacks on innocent people. [[@morse-to-whatsapp|WhatsApp]] began labelling forwarded messages, and in 2019 it limited forwarding to five chats at a time around the world.

Before you share something, ask: who made this, and how do they know? Search for the same story on trusted news sites. If you can't find it, don't pass it on.

{{story:fake-news}}

The Chenab Rail Bridge in Jammu and Kashmir carries trains 359 metres above the river, higher above the water than the [[@eiffel-tower|Eiffel Tower]] is tall. Opened in June 2025, it is the highest railway arch bridge in the world, part of a line linking the Kashmir valley to the rest of India's railways.

Its engineers had to plan for winds of over 200 km/h, earthquakes and freezing temperatures. They studied the rocks on both slopes for years. The steel arch was built out from both sides at once, using cables strung high across the gorge, until the two halves met in the middle in 2021.

{{story:bridges}}

The countdown uses [[@negative-numbers|negative numbers]] in disguise. T-minus 60 seconds is really −60 seconds on a timeline where launch is zero. Each second, the number goes up by one, towards zero. After launch, positive numbers take over.

Engineers plan the countdown by working backwards from zero. If fuel takes three hours to load and must finish an hour before launch, the job starts at T-minus 4 hours. A big launch has hundreds of steps planned this way, all linked like a giant timetable.

{{story:rockets-how}}

India's animation history goes back to the early 1900s, but one of its best-loved early works is *Ek Anek Aur Ekta*, a short film from 1974 shown again and again on Doordarshan. It used a bundle of sticks to show that people are stronger together, and its song is still remembered by many parents.

In 1992, Indian and Japanese artists worked together on *Ramayana: The Legend of Prince Rama*, a full-length animated film. The Indian animator Ram Mohan, often called the father of Indian animation, was one of its leaders.

{{story:ramayana}}

सदियों तक जिन डॉक्टरों ने एक व्यक्ति का खून दूसरे को चढ़ाने की कोशिश की, उन्होंने देखा कि कभी इससे फ़ायदा होता है और कभी जान चली जाती है। किसी को नहीं पता था कि क्यों। 1901 में ऑस्ट्रिया के वैज्ञानिक कार्ल लैंडस्टाइनर ने वियना की अपनी प्रयोगशाला में अपने साथियों का खून आपस में मिलाकर देखा। कुछ [[sample|नमूनों]] में गुच्छे बन गए, कुछ में नहीं। उन्होंने इसका पैटर्न खोज निकाला, जो आगे चलकर ABO ग्रुप बना।

उनकी खोज से सुरक्षित ढंग से खून चढ़ाना मुमकिन हुआ, और इसके लिए उन्हें 1930 में नोबेल पुरस्कार मिला। 1940 में उन्होंने अमेरिका में एक साथी के साथ मिलकर आरएच फ़ैक्टर खोजा।

{{story:mumbai}}

1860 के दशक में कार्ल वुंडरलिख नाम के एक जर्मन डॉक्टर ने अपने मरीज़ों के तापमान की ढेरों रीडिंग इकट्ठा कीं। उन्होंने तय किया कि सेहतमंद लोगों का [[@averages|औसत]] तापमान 37 °C होता है, और यही अंक दुनिया भर की किताबों में बैठ गया।

पर सामान्य तापमान कोई एक पक्का अंक नहीं है। सुबह-सुबह तुम्हारा तापमान कम होता है और देर दोपहर में ज़्यादा। मुँह, बगल और कान में यह थोड़ा-थोड़ा अलग होता है। 2020 में स्टैनफ़र्ड यूनिवर्सिटी की एक टीम ने 1860 के दशक तक के अमेरिकी रिकॉर्ड देखे। उन्हें लगा कि औसत तापमान थोड़ा नीचे आ गया है। एक संभावित वजह यह है कि आज लोगों को लंबे समय तक चलने वाले [[infection|संक्रमण]] कम होते हैं, इसलिए उनका शरीर ज़रा ठंडा चलता है। दूसरे वैज्ञानिक इस विचार को अभी परख रहे हैं।

{{story:thermometer}}

इतिहासकार मानते हैं कि संहिता के सबसे पुराने हिस्से 2,000 साल से भी काफ़ी पहले के हैं, पर यह किताब एक बार में नहीं लिखी गई। बाद के लेखकों ने अध्याय जोड़े और पुराने अध्यायों में फेरबदल किया। एक विद्वान, नागार्जुन, का नाम अक्सर संपादक के रूप में लिया जाता है। आज छपने वाला रूप शायद सबसे पुराने हिस्सों के कई सदियों बाद बना।

इसलिए संहिता किसी एक किताब से ज़्यादा किसी पाठशाला की उस पुस्तिका जैसी है, जिसे गुरु लगातार नया करते रहे।

{{story:sanskrit}}

In 1905, in San Francisco, an 11-year-old named Frank Epperson mixed a fruity soda drink with a stirring stick and left the cup outside one cold night. By morning, it had frozen solid around the stick. He pulled it out and licked it.

He called it the "Epsicle". Years later, in 1923, he applied for a patent, and his own children called it "Pop's 'sicle". That's how the Popsicle got its name. The full tale of that cold night sits with other [[@accident-popsicle|accidental inventions]].

{{story:accident-popsicle}}

In China, people began growing [[@rice|rice]] along the Yangtze River and millet along the Yellow River. In Mexico, they turned teosinte into [[@corn-maize|maize]], and later grew beans and squash beside it. In the Andes of South America, they grew [[@potatoes|potatoes]] and kept llamas. In New Guinea, they grew taro and [[@bananas|bananas]]. In Africa, they grew sorghum and some kinds of [[@millets|millets]].

In South Asia, one of the oldest farming sites is Mehrgarh, in the hills of Balochistan, now in Pakistan. About 9,000 years ago, people there grew barley and wheat and kept humped zebu cattle, the kind still seen across India today. Their [[descendant|descendants]] helped build the [[@indus-cities|Indus cities]].

{{story:soil-made}}

From 520 BC, one of the races was the *hoplitodromos*. Runners wore a bronze helmet and carried a heavy round shield, and early on they wore leg guards too. All that metal could weigh well over 10 kg, as heavy as a big bag of rice.

It was meant to train men for war, when soldiers had to run at the enemy in full armour. Pictures on Greek vases show runners dropping their shields, and other racers tripping over them.

{{story:olympics}}

Abraham Lincoln was the tallest president, at about 193 cm. James Madison was the shortest, at around 163 cm, about the height of a Class 8 student. Lincoln was also a skilled wrestler as a young man, said to have lost only one match in about 300, and is honoured by the US National Wrestling Hall of Fame.

Lincoln remains the only president with a patent. In 1849, he designed inflatable chambers to lift river boats over shallow sandbanks. It was never built, but his wooden model sits in the Smithsonian. Jimmy Carter, the 39th president, was a peanut farmer before politics. He lived to 100, the longest life of any president, and died in December 2024. He also won the Nobel Peace Prize in 2002, more than 20 years after leaving office.

{{story:president-pm}}

The palaces were built for a hot, dry [[climate|climate]]. Thick walls kept rooms cool. Courtyards opened to the sky. Water flowed everywhere, carried from the hills by channels, into pools and fountains. The sound of water was part of the design. Just up the hill, the sultans had a summer garden called the Generalife, where water jets arch over a long, narrow pool lined with flowers.

In the Court of the Lions, twelve stone lions hold up a fountain basin. Four channels run out from it, dividing the courtyard into quarters. This four-part garden plan, which came from Persia, spread across the Islamic world. The [[@mughals|Mughals]] used a version of it centuries later, around the [[@taj-mahal|Taj Mahal]].

{{story:tessellations}}

The architect, William Emerson, mixed styles. The great central dome and the layout echo European palaces. The corner domes, arches and carved details borrow from Mughal buildings. People call this mix Indo-Saracenic.

The white marble came from Makrana in Rajasthan, the same quarries that supplied the Taj Mahal three centuries earlier. It took fifteen years to raise and finish the building. The main building rises about 56 metres, about the height of an 18-storey block of flats.

{{story:east-india-company}}

The Hitopadesha has four parts. The first is about gaining friends. The second is about losing them, and how clever enemies can break up good friendships. The third is about war, and the fourth is about making peace.

In the first book, a crow, a mouse, a tortoise and a deer become firm friends and keep rescuing each other from danger. In the second, a sly jackal turns a lion against his loyal friend, a bull. The later books follow two kingdoms of birds, the geese and the peacocks, as they fight and then make peace.

{{story:crow-smart}}

Sweets are a way of wishing someone a sweet year. During Diwali, homes fill with laddoos, barfis and kaju katli, and families exchange boxes with neighbours. In Maharashtra, people also make a festive snack platter called *faral*, with crunchy chakli, chivda and sweet karanji.

At [[@holi|Holi]], many north Indian homes fry *gujiya*, little pastry pockets stuffed with khoya and dry fruit, and serve cold, spiced *thandai*. In winter, Makar Sankranti brings sesame and jaggery sweets, handed out with a saying that means "take this and speak sweetly".

{{story:diwali-lights}}
